### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found that while the New York Attorney General's office filed a 2018 civil lawsuit seeking temporary bans on the Trump children from serving as directors of New York charities, the subsequent settlement did not include a permanent prohibition; it resulted in the [dissolution of the Trump Foundation](https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/oct/24/facebook-posts/trump-family-disallowed-operating-charities-new-yo/), a [$2 million payment from Donald Trump](https://www.commondreams.org/news/2019/11/07/2-million-and-compulsory-training-presidents-children-called-poetic-end-trump), and mandatory training, with [no evidence of a permanent ban](https://www.commondreams.org/news/2019/11/07/2-million-and-compulsory-training-presidents-children-called-poetic-end-trump). The allegations concerned [mismanagement and self-dealing within the Eric Trump Foundation](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/), not "stealing" donations in a criminal sense, and these were not proven as legal liability in court, leading to the verdict that the claim is refuted.